Harbor: mshtml: only the document itself may ask about a certificate (#100).

urlmon shows wininet's "Security Warning" dialog for a certificate problem unless the download was started
with BINDF_NO_UI, and mshtml started every download without it. A page then got a modal dialog for each
script, image or background request to a host with a bad certificate. Browsers ask (or refuse) for the page
itself and let such a request fail quietly.

Office's add-in Store page sends reports to csp.microsoft.com, which presents a certificate for
*.azureedge.net (checked outside Wine too: the names really don't match). The dialog came up over the Store
window on every visit, and answering "Yes" would have accepted the certificate.

Downloads other than the document itself now carry BINDF_NO_UI: the request fails with the certificate
error and nothing is asked. No test: it needs a host with a wrong certificate.

--- a/dlls/mshtml/navigate.c
+++ b/dlls/mshtml/navigate.c
@@ -1907,6 +1907,11 @@
     bindf = BINDF_ASYNCHRONOUS | BINDF_ASYNCSTORAGE | BINDF_PULLDATA;
     if(post_data_size)
         bindf |= BINDF_FORMS_SUBMIT | BINDF_PRAGMA_NO_CACHE | BINDF_HYPERLINK | BINDF_GETNEWESTVERSION;
+    /* HARBOR HACK: only the document itself may ask the user about a certificate. A script, an image or a
+     * background request that can't be trusted just fails, as in a browser, instead of putting a dialog
+     * over the page for each such request. */
+    if(!is_doc_binding)
+        bindf |= BINDF_NO_UI;
 
     init_bscallback(&ret->bsc, &nsChannelBSCVtbl, mon, bindf);
     ret->is_doc_channel = is_doc_binding;
